          "citation": "2017 UT App 94, 400 P.3d 1078",
          "court": "Court of Appeals of Utah",
          "year": 2017,
          "date_decided": "2017-06-08",
          "holding": "The court affirmed the juvenile court's substantiation of the DCFS supported finding that the mother failed to protect her child from a known sex offender, holding the finding was supported by the petition's deemed-admitted allegations and the court's own findings.",
          "relevance": "The safety plan was the non-court alternative first tried; its breach is what drove the case to petition.",
          "quote": "Because \"a foundation for the court's decision exists in the evidence,\" this court \"may not engage in a reweighing of the evidence.\"",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/5345586/kc-v-state/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 5345586
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In re K.F.",
          "citation": "2009 UT 4, 201 P.3d 985",
          "court": "Utah Supreme Court",
          "year": 2009,
          "date_decided": "2009-01-23",
          "holding": "The Court affirmed, holding that the juvenile court had subject matter jurisdiction because the mother voluntarily placed K.F. in state custody, that the order ending reunification and setting a goal of individualized permanency was final and appealable and appropriate where compelling reasons ruled out reunification, adoption, guardianship and kinship placement, and that the juvenile court's findings on service-plan compliance and on DCFS's reasonable efforts were owed broad discretion and were not clearly in error.",
          "relevance": "It shows the juvenile court deploying protective supervision services in the home before any change of custody, and confirms the court's continuing authority over that non-removal disposition.",
          "quote": "appellate courts defer to juvenile courts on matters of credibility, and juvenile courts have broad discretion in determining whether reasonable reunification efforts were made",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2634909/state-ex-rel-kf/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 2634909
        },
        {
          "case_name": "S.A. v. State",
          "citation": "2016 UT App 191, 382 P.3d 642",
          "court": "Court of Appeals of Utah",
          "year": 2016,
          "date_decided": "2016-09-09",
          "holding": "The court affirmed a disposition order requiring a father to complete a domestic violence assessment, holding that once a child is adjudicated dependent the juvenile court has continuing jurisdiction and may impose any condition on a parent that is reasonable, proportionate, and calculated to serve the child's best interest.",
          "relevance": "Protective supervision in the home is the least intrusive dispositional option, the court-supervised analogue to alternative response.",
          "quote": "Under section 78A-6-117(2), the juvenile court may impose conditions on a parent so long as the conditions are \"reasonable.\"",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/6655746/sa-v-state/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 6655746
        }
